1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,910
June seventh, nineteen forty-two.

2
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:07,430
It's Sunday on Attu, the last island at
the far western end of America.

3
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:11,030
The villagers are in church when
Japanese soldiers arrive.

4
00:00:12,154 --> 00:00:14,104
Within months, the village is gone.

5
00:00:14,354 --> 00:00:16,784
About forty of its people are
shipped to Japan.

6
00:00:17,194 --> 00:00:18,884
About half of them never come back.

7
00:00:19,767 --> 00:00:22,017
Today, Attu has nobody at all.

8
00:00:22,067 --> 00:00:23,397
And it's not the only one.

9
00:00:23,767 --> 00:00:27,317
Adak, a few hundred miles east, was a
Navy town of thousands.

10
00:00:27,787 --> 00:00:31,557
Last year, local news counted only a few
dozen people there in winter.

11
00:00:32,816 --> 00:00:35,586
So why do America's Aleutian
Islands keep emptying?

12
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:38,703
The first answer seems obvious: the war.

13
00:00:38,873 --> 00:00:42,663
But Adak grew after the war, to more than
four and a half thousand people.

14
00:00:42,893 --> 00:00:44,063
Only then did it empty.

15
00:00:45,242 --> 00:00:46,772
The second answer: the weather.

16
00:00:46,922 --> 00:00:49,752
Wind almost every day, cloud
most of the time.

17
00:00:49,862 --> 00:00:52,862
But people lived on these islands
for nine thousand years,

18
00:00:52,902 --> 00:00:54,252
in the same storms.

19
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:56,510
So it's something else.

20
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,790
To find it, we'll first try to get there.

21
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,640
Then we'll go back to nineteen forty-two,

22
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:03,010
to the village that never came home.

23
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:05,770
Then we'll visit the Navy town that
turned off the lights.

24
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:08,660
And at the end, we'll see what really
empties these islands,

25
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:10,870
and why it's starting to change right now.

26
00:01:12,752 --> 00:01:17,092
The Aleutians run from the tip of the Alaska
Peninsula far out into the Pacific,

27
00:01:17,132 --> 00:01:19,382
for more than a thousand miles.

28
00:01:19,792 --> 00:01:23,102
Laid along the East Coast, they would
reach from New York to Miami.

29
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:26,290
They are a chain of volcanoes.

30
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,860
One of them, Great Sitkin,
next door to Adak,

31
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:31,190
has been oozing lava since
twenty twenty-one.

32
00:01:32,409 --> 00:01:35,959
The last island, Attu, is fifteen
hundred miles from Anchorage.

33
00:01:36,329 --> 00:01:37,539
That's London to Moscow.

34
00:01:38,349 --> 00:01:42,059
Russia's Kamchatka is less than five
hundred and fifty miles away.

35
00:01:42,309 --> 00:01:44,959
Nearly three times closer to
Russia than to Anchorage.

36
00:01:46,192 --> 00:01:47,982
Getting there is the first test.

37
00:01:48,312 --> 00:01:50,542
Adak has two flights a week
from Anchorage.

38
00:01:51,032 --> 00:01:53,252
The state ferry turns around
at Dutch Harbor,

39
00:01:53,292 --> 00:01:54,982
about once a month in summer.

40
00:01:55,212 --> 00:01:58,122
West of Adak, there is no
scheduled service at all.

41
00:01:59,179 --> 00:02:00,709
And the weather sets the price.

42
00:02:01,159 --> 00:02:05,059
On Amchitka, in the middle of the chain, the
wind averages twenty-two to thirty

43
00:02:05,099 --> 00:02:08,609
miles an hour, and it is calm less than
one percent of the time.

44
00:02:09,039 --> 00:02:12,719
Adak's airport sign once welcomed visitors
to the Birthplace of the Winds,

45
00:02:12,759 --> 00:02:14,929
until the wind blew half the sign away.

46
00:02:15,568 --> 00:02:18,558
People here have a word for the
worst of it: williwaw.

47
00:02:18,688 --> 00:02:22,598
A sudden blast of cold air that falls off
the mountains onto the sea.

48
00:02:23,616 --> 00:02:26,046
But wind like that never
emptied these islands.

49
00:02:26,256 --> 00:02:27,226
Something else did.

50
00:02:27,456 --> 00:02:30,046
It arrived by ship, in
seventeen forty-five.

51
00:02:33,204 --> 00:02:36,614
And yet, people lived here long
before any plane or ferry.

52
00:02:36,964 --> 00:02:40,454
The oldest site in the chain is about
nine thousand years old.

53
00:02:41,353 --> 00:02:44,153
The Unangan, the people the
Russians called Aleuts,

54
00:02:44,193 --> 00:02:49,123
hunted seals and sea otters from kayaks of
sealskin stretched over driftwood and bone.

55
00:02:49,513 --> 00:02:52,003
They lived in houses dug
halfway into the ground,

56
00:02:52,113 --> 00:02:52,803
out of the wind.

57
00:02:53,233 --> 00:02:55,283
Many still use the name Aleut today.

58
00:02:56,416 --> 00:03:00,466
Before outsiders came, there were maybe
fifteen to eighteen thousand of them.

59
00:03:00,636 --> 00:03:02,586
They needed nothing from anywhere else.

60
00:03:02,796 --> 00:03:04,006
The islands fed them.

61
00:03:04,987 --> 00:03:10,057
That changed in seventeen forty-five, when
Russian fur hunters landed on Attu.

62
00:03:10,307 --> 00:03:12,557
Within weeks, they had killed fifteen men.

63
00:03:13,007 --> 00:03:15,397
A bay on Attu is still
called Massacre Bay.

64
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:20,950
The hunters wanted sea otter fur, and they
forced the Unangan to hunt it for them.

65
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:23,990
Violence, hunger and new
diseases followed.

66
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:28,550
By the end of Russian rule, the Unangan had lost
nearly eighty percent of their people.

67
00:03:29,731 --> 00:03:32,241
And from then on, the islands
lived on ships.

68
00:03:32,711 --> 00:03:36,761
In eighteen eleven, a visitor found the
company post on Attu failing.

69
00:03:37,071 --> 00:03:39,221
No ship had stopped there for five years.

70
00:03:40,347 --> 00:03:45,817
In eighteen thirty-one, a priest named Netsvetov
counted a hundred and twenty people on Attu.

71
00:03:46,553 --> 00:03:49,863
After America bought Alaska in
eighteen sixty-seven,

72
00:03:50,093 --> 00:03:52,363
services to the remote islands shrank.

73
00:03:52,653 --> 00:03:55,413
Within about ten years, Attu's
population halved,

74
00:03:55,453 --> 00:03:57,903
from two hundred and twenty to a
hundred and seven.

75
00:03:59,305 --> 00:04:03,735
By the summer of nineteen forty-two,
forty-five Unangan were left on Attu.

76
00:04:03,945 --> 00:04:06,635
That summer, the war reached
them from both sides.

77
00:04:09,687 --> 00:04:14,017
In June nineteen forty-two, Japanese
carrier planes bombed Dutch Harbor.

78
00:04:14,427 --> 00:04:17,977
Days later, Japanese troops landed
on Kiska and on Attu.

79
00:04:18,992 --> 00:04:22,122
On Attu, the teacher's husband,
Charles Foster Jones,

80
00:04:22,252 --> 00:04:24,562
radioed the weather to Dutch
Harbor every day.

81
00:04:24,772 --> 00:04:26,502
He died within a day of the landing.

82
00:04:26,852 --> 00:04:28,722
The Japanese said it was suicide.

83
00:04:29,152 --> 00:04:31,442
When his body was dug up in
nineteen forty-eight,

84
00:04:31,792 --> 00:04:33,162
he had been shot through the head.

85
00:04:34,276 --> 00:04:38,296
In September, the villagers were loaded into
the hold of a coal ship and taken to

86
00:04:38,336 --> 00:04:39,906
Otaru, in Japan.

87
00:04:40,456 --> 00:04:42,826
Among them was six-year-old Nick Golodoff.

88
00:04:43,747 --> 00:04:45,897
In Japan, they worked and they starved.

89
00:04:46,387 --> 00:04:51,097
A mother named Olean Golodoff fed her children
orange peels to stop them crying.

90
00:04:51,387 --> 00:04:54,197
She lost her husband and three of
her seven children there.

91
00:04:54,407 --> 00:04:56,537
About half of the Attuans died in Japan.

92
00:04:57,822 --> 00:04:59,412
Meanwhile, the U.S.

93
00:04:59,462 --> 00:05:01,452
government removed its own citizens.

94
00:05:02,482 --> 00:05:06,852
Eight hundred and eighty-one Unangan from nine
villages were taken off the islands. On St.

95
00:05:06,982 --> 00:05:10,272
Paul, people got a few hours' notice,
and one suitcase each.

96
00:05:11,423 --> 00:05:12,893
Greg Fratis was two.

97
00:05:13,003 --> 00:05:15,073
Years later, he remembered
the elders' words:

98
00:05:15,323 --> 00:05:17,173
one suitcase, the boat is here.

99
00:05:17,463 --> 00:05:20,433
At seventy-seven, he said: let's
not let it happen again.

100
00:05:21,556 --> 00:05:25,236
On Atka, American servicemen set the
homes and the church on fire,

101
00:05:25,276 --> 00:05:27,266
so the Japanese couldn't use them.

102
00:05:28,159 --> 00:05:32,759
The evacuees were put in an old cannery, a
herring plant and an abandoned mine camp

103
00:05:32,799 --> 00:05:36,749
in Southeast Alaska, with rotten floors
and little medical care.

104
00:05:37,059 --> 00:05:39,449
At least seventy-four people
died in the camps.

105
00:05:40,073 --> 00:05:42,903
But the strangest battle out here
was still to come.

106
00:05:43,093 --> 00:05:45,283
And it was won by cutting a supply line.

107
00:05:48,104 --> 00:05:52,984
In March nineteen forty-three, American ships
fought a four-hour gun battle near the

108
00:05:53,024 --> 00:05:56,854
Komandorski Islands and turned back a
Japanese supply convoy.

109
00:05:57,344 --> 00:06:01,654
After that, Japan never again sent surface
ships to supply the Aleutians.

110
00:06:02,753 --> 00:06:06,983
Two months later, fifteen thousand American
soldiers landed on Attu in fog.

111
00:06:07,373 --> 00:06:10,943
The fight ended in one of the largest
banzai charges of the war.

112
00:06:11,173 --> 00:06:14,403
Of about twenty-five hundred Japanese,
only twenty-nine survived.

113
00:06:14,793 --> 00:06:16,793
About five hundred and fifty
Americans died,

114
00:06:16,833 --> 00:06:19,203
and twelve hundred more were
hurt by the cold.

115
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:22,230
Japan is still bringing its dead home.

116
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:25,550
In twenty twenty-four, a team dug
up two more soldiers.

117
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,010
Fog and snow leave only July
and August to dig.

118
00:06:30,116 --> 00:06:31,346
Then came Kiska.

119
00:06:31,456 --> 00:06:35,966
In August, more than thirty-four thousand American
and Canadian troops stormed the island.

120
00:06:36,678 --> 00:06:37,548
It was empty.

121
00:06:37,658 --> 00:06:42,288
Cut off from supplies, the Japanese had slipped away
in the fog more than two weeks earlier.

122
00:06:43,288 --> 00:06:47,538
Even so, about three hundred Allied men
were killed or wounded there.

123
00:06:47,708 --> 00:06:50,298
By friendly fire in the fog,
by booby traps,

124
00:06:50,348 --> 00:06:53,678
and by a mine that tore open the
destroyer Abner Read.

125
00:06:54,895 --> 00:06:57,925
After the battles, the evacuees
finally came home.

126
00:06:58,195 --> 00:07:01,465
Many found their houses and churches
looted by American troops.

127
00:07:02,508 --> 00:07:04,838
And four villages never came back at all:

128
00:07:05,128 --> 00:07:07,718
Attu, Biorka, Kashega and Makushin.

129
00:07:07,988 --> 00:07:11,018
The government decided they were
too costly to travel to.

130
00:07:12,056 --> 00:07:14,866
The surviving Attuans were sent
to Atka instead.

131
00:07:15,336 --> 00:07:17,706
Nick Golodoff lived there for
the rest of his life.

132
00:07:18,036 --> 00:07:22,346
In nineteen eighty-eight, Congress paid each
surviving evacuee twelve thousand dollars.

133
00:07:25,848 --> 00:07:27,938
After the war, the military stayed.

134
00:07:28,208 --> 00:07:31,158
And Adak became one of the
strangest towns in America.

135
00:07:32,182 --> 00:07:36,002
In nineteen ninety, the Navy base had more than
four and a half thousand people,

136
00:07:36,042 --> 00:07:38,532
and at its peak nearly six thousand.

137
00:07:38,702 --> 00:07:41,752
It had a McDonald's, a Pizza
Hut, a movie theater,

138
00:07:41,882 --> 00:07:43,052
even a ski area.

139
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:46,070
It even had a national forest.

140
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:50,800
During the war, the story goes, soldiers planted
a few trees on the treeless island,

141
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:51,950
to lift morale.

142
00:07:52,260 --> 00:07:53,110
Most of them died.

143
00:07:53,420 --> 00:07:57,550
Someone put up a sign: You are now entering and
leaving the Adak National Forest.

144
00:07:58,854 --> 00:08:00,224
Then the Cold War ended.

145
00:08:00,394 --> 00:08:02,404
In nineteen ninety-seven, the base closed.

146
00:08:03,245 --> 00:08:06,855
By two thousand, Adak had three
hundred and sixteen people.

147
00:08:07,185 --> 00:08:10,395
By twenty twenty, about a
hundred and seventy.

148
00:08:10,435 --> 00:08:11,495
A ninety-six percent drop.

149
00:08:11,705 --> 00:08:15,335
If New York shrank like that, it would be a
city of three hundred thousand.

150
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:19,190
Buildings made for thousands
now stand empty.

151
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:22,670
In one recent school year, Adak's
school had five students.

152
00:08:23,674 --> 00:08:25,444
The town tried to live on fish.

153
00:08:25,674 --> 00:08:28,104
Its plant once had up to
three hundred jobs,

154
00:08:28,234 --> 00:08:30,304
and it depended on a federal cod quota.

155
00:08:30,674 --> 00:08:33,464
In twenty nineteen, a court
struck the quota down.

156
00:08:33,674 --> 00:08:35,324
The plant closed the next year.

157
00:08:36,403 --> 00:08:39,693
Then, in October twenty
twenty-five, Adak's power failed.

158
00:08:40,023 --> 00:08:41,753
Its only backup generator broke.

159
00:08:41,963 --> 00:08:44,453
The replacement was on a barge
in the Bering Sea.

160
00:08:44,763 --> 00:08:48,403
To keep the school and clinic running, the town
borrowed a generator from its own

161
00:08:48,443 --> 00:08:49,553
closed fish plant.

162
00:08:49,843 --> 00:08:52,753
For seventeen days, parts of the town
lived without electricity.

163
00:08:53,877 --> 00:08:58,527
One resident, Steven Carroll, ran his own
generator and cooked for three families.

164
00:08:58,977 --> 00:09:03,091
"I don't know how long we can get
by," he said. And Attu?

165
00:09:03,131 --> 00:09:06,091
In twenty ten, the last twenty-one
people on the island,

166
00:09:06,131 --> 00:09:09,761
all Coast Guard, left when their
navigation station closed.

167
00:09:10,211 --> 00:09:12,521
Even the closing ceremony ran a day late:

168
00:09:12,851 --> 00:09:14,081
fog kept the plane away.

169
00:09:17,250 --> 00:09:18,520
So what empties the Aleutians?

170
00:09:19,176 --> 00:09:20,386
Not the weather alone.

171
00:09:20,556 --> 00:09:23,906
For nine thousand years, people lived
here on what the sea gave them.

172
00:09:24,681 --> 00:09:28,911
But since seventeen forty-five, the islands
have lived on a line from outside:

173
00:09:29,281 --> 00:09:31,791
fur ships, government
services, military money,

174
00:09:31,961 --> 00:09:34,251
a fish quota, a barge with a generator.

175
00:09:34,601 --> 00:09:36,951
And the first ships didn't just
supply the islands.

176
00:09:37,301 --> 00:09:38,231
They brought the collapse.

177
00:09:38,918 --> 00:09:40,868
The weather sets the price of that line.

178
00:09:41,038 --> 00:09:42,548
The line decides who stays.

179
00:09:42,878 --> 00:09:45,088
Every time it was cut, the
islands emptied.

180
00:09:45,518 --> 00:09:47,148
Attu after Alaska was sold.

181
00:09:47,238 --> 00:09:49,008
The lost villages after the war.

182
00:09:49,138 --> 00:09:51,778
The Japanese on Kiska in
nineteen forty-three.

183
00:09:51,818 --> 00:09:53,708
Adak in the nineteen nineties.

184
00:09:54,192 --> 00:09:56,742
The one town that holds on
is Dutch Harbor.

185
00:09:57,032 --> 00:09:59,632
It's America's biggest fishing
port by volume,

186
00:09:59,672 --> 00:10:01,202
twenty-six years in a row.

187
00:10:01,392 --> 00:10:06,582
But across these islands, about four in five
seafood-plant workers come from outside Alaska.

188
00:10:08,059 --> 00:10:10,049
And now the line is coming back.

189
00:10:10,379 --> 00:10:15,079
In April twenty twenty-six, the Navy and a
federal agency set aside up to a hundred

190
00:10:15,119 --> 00:10:17,829
and fifteen million dollars
for Adak's power,

191
00:10:17,979 --> 00:10:19,509
runway, port and fuel.

192
00:10:20,707 --> 00:10:25,527
In October twenty twenty-six, American fighter
jets stood on alert on Shemya for the

193
00:10:25,567 --> 00:10:27,017
first time in decades.

194
00:10:27,747 --> 00:10:31,527
Chinese and Russian warships have sailed past
these islands in recent years,

195
00:10:31,567 --> 00:10:33,117
in international waters.

196
00:10:34,335 --> 00:10:36,065
So the line is being rebuilt.

197
00:10:36,375 --> 00:10:39,865
Not for the people who left, but because
someone needs these islands again.

198
00:10:40,801 --> 00:10:45,471
Would you live on Adak for one year, with two
flights a week and a generator on a barge?

199
00:10:45,701 --> 00:10:46,671
Tell me in the comments.

200
00:10:47,021 --> 00:10:49,471
And tell me which place we
should explain next.

201
00:10:50,527 --> 00:10:54,117
On that Sunday in nineteen forty-two, the
villagers of Attu were in church.

202
00:10:54,547 --> 00:10:56,517
Today, nobody lives on the island.

203
00:10:56,807 --> 00:11:00,187
But some summers, when the fog lifts, a
refuge ship brings a few of their

204
00:11:00,227 --> 00:11:02,777
descendants back, to stand where
their village was.

205
00:11:03,007 --> 00:11:05,917
About three hundred Attuan
descendants live in America today.

206
00:11:06,187 --> 00:11:07,257
This was How It's Run.
